 '| Safe at Home |§9E.6 separately sends program ballots through the state commissioner; it permits receipt before pollclose or qualifying prior-day postmark/barcode, followed by certification/forwarding. That section does not itself supply the same Monday-noon terminal date as§53.44. Do not invent one or treat the ordinary county cutoff as universal. | '+citation('law-9E.6','§9E.6')+' |',
 '| Provisional evidence and count | Required proof under§49.81(6) is due by noon Monday after this election, subject to its earlier-canvass exception. Public provisional list due by9am second day (November5); special board reconvenes no earlier than noon second day, with specified holiday/earlier-canvass exceptions. Accepted/rejected counts and later timely absentee canvass remain separate. | '+citation('law-49.81','§49.81')+', '+citation('law-50.20','§50.20')+', '+citation('law-50.21','§50.21')+', '+citation('law-50.22','§50.22')+' |',
 '| County canvass/correction | Current§50.24 says first Tuesday after election, ordinarilyNovember10. Board includes qualifying later counts, certifies precinct contest summary with scattering/under/over where applicable, and records clerical corrections in minutes. Older Monday-or-Tuesday summaries are stale. | '+citation('law-50.24','§50.24')+' |',
 '| Federal recount | Current§50.48(1)(c) uses written request by5pm day following county canvass and a gap strictly below0.15% for statewide/federal office. State commissioner orders start within7days of receipt; board convening,18-day reporting, correction and delayed state-canvass provisions remain in the section. It is a requested statutory process, not an automatic threshold-triggered count. | '+citation('law-50.48','§50.48')+' |',
